          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Bartholin's glands drain into which anatomical space of the female external geni</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Bartholin's glands drain into which anatomical space of the female external genitalia?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Vaginal canal</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Bartholin's glands do not drain into the vaginal canal. Their ducts open into the vestibule — the cleft between the labia minora — at the 5 and 7 o'clock positions relative to the vaginal orifice.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Vestibule</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Bartholin's gland ducts open into the vestibule, which is the space enclosed between the labia minora. The vestibule also contains the openings of the urethral meatus and the vaginal orifice.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Posterior fornix</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The posterior fornix is an intravaginal recess between the cervix and the posterior vaginal wall — it is not part of the external genitalia. Bartholin's glands open into the vestibule.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Mons pubis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The mons pubis is the fatty pad overlying the pubic symphysis and has no glandular drainage into it. Bartholin's gland ducts open into the vestibule between the labia minora.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The vestibule — the cleft between the labia minora — is where Bartholin's gland ducts open, along with the urethral meatus and the vaginal orifice."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. The vestibule receives the openings of Bartholin's glands, the urethra, and the vagina — making it a clinically important landmark during perineal examination."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
