          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>During an IVF cycle, the embryologist observes a solid ball of 16 or more cells </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>During an IVF cycle, the embryologist observes a solid ball of 16 or more cells with no fluid-filled cavity. At what stage of pre-implantation development is this embryo?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Zygote</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The zygote is the single-celled stage formed immediately after fertilisation. A solid mass of 16+ cells has already undergone several cleavage divisions and is at the morula stage.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Blastocyst</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The blastocyst stage is characterised by the appearance of a fluid-filled cavity (blastocoel) around day 5. A solid ball without a cavity is still at the morula stage.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Morula</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The morula is a solid ball of 16 or more cells (blastomeres) formed by day 3 to 4 of development. The absence of a fluid-filled cavity distinguishes it from the blastocyst, which forms when the blastocoel appears around day 5.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Trophoblast</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The trophoblast is the outer cell layer of the blastocyst that will form placental tissues — it is not a developmental stage. The solid 16+ cell mass is called the morula.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "A solid ball of 16+ cells without a fluid-filled cavity is the morula (day 3–4). The blastocyst forms later (day 5) when the blastocoel cavity appears."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. The morula (day 3–4) is a solid mass of 16+ blastomeres. Fluid accumulation transforms it into a blastocyst (day 5), which is the stage capable of implanting into the endometrium."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
