        {
          "duration": {
            "from": 433.69999999999993,
            "to": 443.69999999999993
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>During a bimanual examination, a gynaecologist palpates a nodular, indurated are</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>During a bimanual examination, a gynaecologist palpates a nodular, indurated area through the posterior fornix. Which structure is most likely the site of pathology?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Urinary bladder</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The bladder lies anterior to the uterus and is assessed through the anterior fornix or by abdominal palpation, not through the posterior fornix.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pouch of Douglas</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The Pouch of Douglas (rectouterine pouch) is separated from the posterior fornix by only a thin vaginal wall, making it directly palpable. Induration here suggests endometriosis on the uterosacral ligaments or metastatic deposits.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Broad ligament</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The broad ligament is a lateral peritoneal fold and is not directly palpable through the posterior fornix. The posterior fornix overlies the Pouch of Douglas.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Uterine isthmus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The isthmus connects the uterine body to the cervix and is palpated anteriorly. Nodularity through the posterior fornix points to the Pouch of Douglas, which lies directly behind it.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The Pouch of Douglas lies immediately behind the posterior fornix, separated only by a thin wall. Induration palpated through the posterior fornix most likely originates in the Pouch of Douglas."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. The posterior fornix is the key clinical window to the Pouch of Douglas — induration or nodularity here is a hallmark of deep infiltrating endometriosis or malignant deposits on the uterosacral ligaments."
                }
              ],
